---
import ExamplePlan from '../../components/content/ExamplePlan.astro';
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import { examplesFor } from '../../lib/guides/examples';
import { len, range } from '../../lib/guides/measure';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/paths';

const slug = 'home-office-layout';
---

<Guide slug={slug}>
  <p class="text-lg text-slate-600">
    A home office has one job: a desk you can sit at comfortably for hours, with room to move and
    good light. That decides most of the layout. Here are the sizes and clearances to plan with, and
    example floor plans you can open and adjust.
  </p>

  <h2 id="desk-sizes">Desk sizes</h2>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Desk</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical size</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Compact (laptop, small space)</th>
          <td>
            about {range(36, 48)} wide, {range(20, 24)} deep
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Standard (one monitor)</th>
          <td>
            about {range(48, 60)} wide, {range(24, 30)} deep
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Large (two monitors, paperwork)</th>
          <td>
            about {range(60, 72)} wide, {range(30, 36)} deep
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Height (sitting)</th>
          <td>about {range(28, 30)} for most adults</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Depth matters more than it looks: a monitor needs to sit at least {len(20)} from your eyes, so a
    desk shallower than about {len(24)} pushes it too close.
  </p>

  <h2 id="clearances">Chair and walkway clearances</h2>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Where</th>
          <th scope="col">Clear space</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Behind the desk, to roll the chair back and stand up</th>
          <td>at least {len(36)}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Behind the desk if people also walk behind you</th>
          <td>{range(44, 48)}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Office chair footprint</th>
          <td>about {range(24, 28)} across the base</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">In front of a filing cabinet, to open a drawer</th>
          <td>about {len(36)}</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="screen">Monitor distance and height</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Distance:</strong> OSHA's workstation guidance puts the screen at least {len(20)} from
      your eyes, usually {range(20, 40)}, about an arm's length.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Height:</strong> the top of the screen at or slightly below eye level.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="window">Where to put the desk</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Window to the side.</strong> A desk at right angles to the window gives you daylight
      without glare on the screen (window behind you) or squinting into it (window behind the
      screen).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Facing the room</strong> (back to a wall) feels less boxed in and puts a plain wall
      behind you on video calls, but needs more floor.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Facing a wall</strong> uses the least floor and suits a small room or a desk in a
      corner of another room.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keep the door swing clear</strong> of the chair's path, and plan an outlet near the
      desk.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="examples">Example home office layouts</h2>
  {examplesFor(slug).map((e) => (
    <ExamplePlan example={e} />
  ))}

  <p>
    Short on space? See the{' '}
    <a href={withBase('guides/studio-and-dorm-layout/')}>studio and dorm layout guide</a>, or the{' '}
    <a href={withBase('guides/living-room-layout/')}>living room guide</a>.
  </p>
</Guide>
